---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ApiKeyGenerator from '../../components/ApiKeyGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="API Key Generator" description="Generate a random API key with a custom prefix and length, for local testing or seeding.">
  <ApiKeyGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why generate fake API keys</h2>
  <p>When building or testing a system that issues API keys, you often need realistic-looking sample keys before your real key-issuing logic exists — for seeding a database, writing tests, or mocking an integration.</p>

  <h2>Features</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Custom prefix (e.g. <code>sk_test_</code>) to mimic real provider key formats</li>
    <li>Adjustable length</li>
    <li>Cryptographically random character generation</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Set your desired prefix and length.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated key.</li>
    <li>Use it as placeholder/test data — not as a real production credential.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>With prefix <code>sk_test_</code> and length 24, you might get <code>sk_test_4f8a2c91b7e3d0f6a1c8</code>.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using a generated key as a real credential.</strong> This tool produces random strings for testing, not cryptographically issued, database-backed API keys with real authorization behind them.</li>
    <li><strong>Committing sample keys to a public repo</strong> without clearly marking them as test/fake values, which can cause confusion later.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Seeding a test database with realistic-looking API key records</li>
    <li>Writing unit tests that need a plausible key format</li>
    <li>Mocking a third-party API response that includes a key field</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this cryptographically secure enough for production?</strong> The randomness is browser-generated and reasonably strong, but production API keys should be generated and tracked server-side with proper issuance and revocation logic — not copy-pasted from a browser tool.</p>
</ToolLayout>
